<Project>

  <!-- ============================================================
       Explains a known Visual Studio toolchain bug that otherwise
       shows up as an opaque NativeAOT publish failure, and also
       covers the plain case where the C++ build toolchain is not
       installed at all.

       On a machine with the environment variable
       NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath set, `dotnet publish` for a
       PublishAot project can fail with MSB3073 and a corrupted
       CppLinker value containing text like:

         'vswhere.exe' is not recognized as an internal or external
         command, operable program or batch file.

       Chain of causes (verified 2026-08-25, machine has the variable
       set): the ILCompiler targets locate the native C++ toolchain by
       running vcvarsall.bat, which calls VsDevCmd.bat. VsDevCmd.bat
       pushd's into the Visual Studio Installer folder and invokes
       vswhere.exe by its bare name, relying on cmd.exe resolving it
       from the current directory. NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath
       disables exactly that resolution, so the lookup fails and
       writes an error to stderr. findvcvarsall.bat redirects only
       stdout, and the ILCompiler targets' ConsoleToMSBuild capture
       folds that stderr text into the CppLinker property. The
       resulting error blames a missing platform linker on a machine
       where the linker is installed and correct.

       This is a Visual Studio bug, not a FalkForge bug. This target
       only detects and explains the condition; it never clears or
       overrides the variable itself. It is a deliberate
       machine-hardening setting, and silently working around a
       security control to make a build convenient is the wrong move
       here.

       Firing condition (measured 2026-08-25 against a real publish,
       see NativeAotPublishDiagnosticsTargetsTests): hooks
       AfterTargets="SetupOSSpecificProps", the ILCompiler target that
       sets $(CppLinker) for the current publish. It is defined only
       in the OS-specific ILCompiler targets
       (Microsoft.NETCore.Native.Windows.targets /
       Microsoft.NETCore.Native.Unix.targets), which are imported only
       when PublishAot pulls in the ILCompiler package, so this never
       runs on an ordinary project. LinkNative depends on IlcCompile,
       which depends on SetupOSSpecificProps, so the hook always lands
       before the linker actually runs. ComputeLinkedFilesToPublish
       (which pulls in LinkNative) only ever runs as part of the
       publish pipeline, never from a plain `dotnet build`, so no
       PublishAot/_IsPublishing gate is needed to keep this off an
       ordinary build.

       The condition itself checks the symptom directly:
       $(CppLinker) resolved to a non-empty value that is not an
       existing file. That is true whenever ILCompiler could not
       locate a working linker, whatever the cause: the corrupted
       lookup above, or the C++ build toolchain simply not being
       installed. The message below names both and, where it can,
       narrows to the one this machine's environment makes more
       likely.

       Excludes IlcUseEnvironmentalTools=true (measured 2026-08-25,
       same test machine, real VC Developer environment on PATH):
       that mode skips findvcvarsall.bat and leaves $(CppLinker) at
       its unresolved default, a bare "link" with no directory. The
       later link step still finds it fine via a normal PATH search,
       so the publish succeeds, but Exists() only checks the
       filesystem, not PATH, so without this exclusion the bare name
       would always read as "missing" and the warning would fire on
       every successful IlcUseEnvironmentalTools=true publish. There
       is no way to tell, from $(CppLinker) alone, whether that mode's
       PATH search will succeed; only the link step itself can answer
       that, so this diagnostic stays out of its way.
       ============================================================ -->

  <Target Name="_FalkDiagnoseAotLinkerEnv" AfterTargets="SetupOSSpecificProps"
          Condition="'$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' AND '$(IlcUseEnvironmentalTools)' != 'true' AND '$(CppLinker)' != '' AND !Exists('$(CppLinker)')">

    <PropertyGroup>
      <_FalkAotLinkerDiagText>NativeAOT could not resolve a working C++ linker. The resolved CppLinker value ('$(CppLinker)') does not point to an existing file. Two known causes: (1) the C++ build toolchain is not installed -- install the &quot;Desktop development with C++&quot; workload from Build Tools for Visual Studio 2022, then re-run. (2) On a machine with NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath set, a Visual Studio toolchain bug corrupts this same lookup even when the linker is installed and correct: vcvarsall.bat -&gt; VsDevCmd.bat resolves vswhere.exe by bare name from the current directory, and that variable disables exactly that resolution. Workaround for this session only, in PowerShell: $env:NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath = $null ; dotnet publish. Do not disable it machine-wide; it is typically a deliberate hardening control.</_FalkAotLinkerDiagText>
      <_FalkAotLinkerDiagText Condition="'$(NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath)' != ''">NativeAOT could not resolve a working C++ linker (resolved value: '$(CppLinker)', which does not exist). NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath is set on this machine, which is the more likely cause: a Visual Studio toolchain bug corrupts this lookup even when the C++ linker is installed and correct (vcvarsall.bat -&gt; VsDevCmd.bat resolves vswhere.exe by bare name from the current directory, and this variable disables exactly that resolution). This is a Visual Studio bug, not a FalkForge one. Workaround for this session only, in PowerShell: $env:NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath = $null ; dotnet publish. Do not disable it machine-wide; it is typically a deliberate hardening control. If clearing it does not fix the publish, the C++ build toolchain itself -- the &quot;Desktop development with C++&quot; workload from Build Tools for Visual Studio 2022 -- may simply not be installed.</_FalkAotLinkerDiagText>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Warning Code="FALKAOT001" Text="$(_FalkAotLinkerDiagText)" />
  </Target>

</Project>
